+# Motif Neurotech
+
+## Company Profile
+
+**Company Name:** Motif Neurotech, Inc.
+**HQ Location:** Houston, TX, USA
+**Founded:** Exact incorporation year **not confirmed** in this research pass — **[unverified]**. Motif was formed as a spinout through the Rice Biotech Launch Pad by Rice faculty Jacob Robinson and Kaiyuan Yang and physicians Sunil Sheth and Sameer Sheth ([Rice News](https://news.rice.edu/news/2024/rice-biotech-launch-pad-startup-motif-neurotech-closes-series-financing-1875-million)); its Series A closed in January 2024, indicating the company was operating by at least 2022–2023.
+**Flagship product:** **DOT (Digitally-programmable Over-brain Therapeutic) microstimulator**
+**Website:** https://www.motifneurotech.com
+
+---
+
+## Funding
+
+Motif closed an oversubscribed **$18.75 million Series A** in January 2024, led by **Arboretum Ventures**, with participation from KdT Ventures, Satori Neuro, Dolby Family Ventures, re.Mind Capital, Max Hodak (individual investor), Divergent Capital, TMC Innovation, PsyMed Ventures, Empath Ventures, and Capital Factory ([BusinessWire](https://www.businesswire.com/news/home/20240124154216/en/Motif-Neurotech-Raises-$18.75-Million-in-Series-A-Financing-to-Advance-Implantable-Device-for-Treatment-Resistant-Depression); [Rice News](https://news.rice.edu/news/2024/rice-biotech-launch-pad-startup-motif-neurotech-closes-series-financing-1875-million)). No later disclosed funding round was found in this research pass — **[unverified]** whether additional capital has been raised since January 2024.
+
+---
+
+## Clinical Indication
+
+**Disorder Category:** Cognitive Disorders / Neural Interface (mental-health application; also explored for epilepsy per backlog description)
+**Primary Indication:** Treatment-resistant depression (TRD) — defined as major depressive disorder that fails to respond to two or more antidepressant medications ([BusinessWire](https://www.businesswire.com/news/home/20240124154216/en/Motif-Neurotech-Raises-$18.75-Million-in-Series-A-Financing-to-Advance-Implantable-Device-for-Treatment-Resistant-Depression)). This research pass found no confirmed evidence of an active epilepsy program — **[unverified]**.
+
+---
+
+## Target Nerve or Mechanism
+
+**Anatomical Target:** Epidural cortical surface (dorsolateral prefrontal cortex, optimal placement over gyral crowns)
+**Delivery Mechanism:** The **DOT microstimulator** is a millimeter-scale (~9mm wide), wireless, battery-free implant placed in the epidural space (above the dura, not penetrating brain tissue) that delivers bursts of electrical stimulation through the dura to the cortical surface, powered wirelessly via magnetoelectric power transfer developed at Rice University; designed for a ~20-minute outpatient implant procedure ([Rice News](https://news.rice.edu/news/2024/rice-team-demonstrates-miniature-brain-stimulator-humans); [Medical Design & Outsourcing](https://www.medicaldesignandoutsourcing.com/motif-neurotech-dot-brain-pacemaker-neurostimulator-design-depression/)).
+**Biological basis:** Epidural cortical stimulation (ECS) is a peer-reviewed neuromodulation approach; the underlying device physics and cortical field modeling are described in **"Miniature battery-free epidural cortical stimulators"** ([PubMed](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/38608028/)) and a follow-on placement-optimization study ([PubMed](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/42413552/)), both associated with the Rice research group underlying Motif's technology.
+
+---
+
+## Regulatory Status & Clinical Trial
+
+- Motif has conducted **first-in-human brain stimulation** with the DOT device as an early feasibility milestone ([BioSpace](https://www.biospace.com/motif-neurotech-conducts-first-in-human-brain-stimulation-with-millimeter-sized-implantable-device-for-treatment-resistant-depression)).
+- Rice News reports the underlying BCI/stimulator technology **"wins FDA approval for first clinical trial"** ([Rice News, 2026](https://news.rice.edu/news/2026/brain-computer-interface-based-rice-research-wins-fda-approval-first-clinical-trial)) — the exact regulatory pathway (IDE vs. Breakthrough Device Designation) and date were **not independently confirmed against an FDA primary source in this research pass — [unverified]**.
+- No PMA, 510(k), or De Novo clearance was found — device remains investigational/pre-market.
+
+---
+
+## Novelty & Differentiation
+
+- **vs. Neuronetics/BrainsWay (TMS):** Motif's DOT is a fully implanted, always-available device (no daily in-clinic visits required after implantation) vs. TMS's requirement for repeated clinic sessions over weeks.
+- **vs. SetPoint Medical/traditional VNS-style implants:** DOT is dramatically smaller (millimeter-scale) and battery-free, implanted via a short outpatient procedure rather than a more invasive surgical implant with a battery/generator.
+- Novel wireless magnetoelectric power-transfer approach, distinct from RF or inductive coupling used by most existing implanted neurostimulators.
+
+---
+
+## Limitations: Practical & Theoretical
+
+- Still in **early feasibility/first-in-human** stage; no confirmed efficacy data from a controlled trial was found in this research pass — **[unverified]**.
+- Epidural (non-penetrating) stimulation of the cortex is a less spatially precise targeting approach than intracortical or even subdural (ECoG-surface) stimulation — general field-level tradeoff.
+- Reliance on a novel wireless magnetoelectric power-transfer mechanism introduces engineering risk not yet proven at commercial scale.
+- Company funding history beyond the January 2024 Series A is sparsely documented — **[unverified]**.
+
+---
+
+## Market Size
+
+Treatment-resistant depression affects an estimated **1–3% of the US population**, with up to two-thirds of depressed patients remaining symptomatic after first-line treatment; the broader treatment-resistant-depression market was estimated at **$14.48 billion in 2024** ([Market Research Future](https://www.marketresearchfuture.com/reports/treatment-resistant-depression-market-42592)).
+
